  "query_preview": "..\nAs of 228 PM EDT Wednesday...\n\nKEY MESSAGE 1: Isolated to scattered showers and thunderstorms are \nbeginning to move through the area this afternoon as a shortwave \naxis centered over western Ontario slowly pivots east. Upper level \nnorthwesterly zonal flow is set up across New England on the \nperiphery of a stagnant ridge over the Plains and Intermountain \nWest. Marginal instability around 1000J/kg is analyzed by on \nmesoanalysis providing the fuel for showers and storms today. \nAdditionally, effective bulk shear to around 35-40 knots may lead to \nsome locally gusty winds up to 30 MPH under any thunderstorms. \nHowever, given the marginal instability, weaker mid level lapse \nrates, and an apparent cap around 700mb based on satellite cloud \npresentations will keep any severe threat low through this evening. \nBest chances for any stronger storms will be across the St. Lawrence \nValley where the best CAPE gradient is. A few training storms will \nbe possible with storm motion paralleling the mean flow. However, \ndeep moisture will be lacking with PWs only to around an inch. Thus, \nany risk of flooding will be slim. While a few isolated showers may \ncontinue through the overnight hours, any thunderstorm activity is \nexpected to wane beyond sunset.\n\nAnother shortwave is expected to move through tomorrow, with better \naligned forcing for our region. Increasing diurnal instability \naround 1000-1500J/kg focused around the International Border with \nsimilar shear profiles will bring better chances for thunderstorms \nto the region tomorrow afternoon, though a few showers may be \npossible in the mid to late morning with little capping inversion \npresent. Instability will highest in northern Vermont, though \nshowers and thunderstorms anywhere across the northern portions of \nthe CWA will be possible tomorrow. PWs will slightly increase \ntomorrow to near an inch and a quarter, with parallel motion and \ntraining cells. However, again, deep warm cloud depths will be thin \nwith a low risk for any flooding concerns. \n\nTroughing will slide east with surface high pressure building in by \nFriday. More calm and quieter conditions are expected through \nSaturday. Temperatures will be relatively seasonable in the mid to \nupper 70s. A radiational cooling night is increasingly likely Friday \nnight with lows potentially dipping to the upper 40s to low to mid \n50s. A few pocket low 40s may be possible in the decoupled protected \nvalleys of the Adirondacks and eastern Greens.\n\nKEY MESSAGE 2: An area of low pressure enters the region for late \nin the weekend and early next week, bringing another round of \nshowers to the region. The track of the low will determine the exact \nimpacts. Right now, a track slightly south is favored, bringing a \nperiod of steady rain. However, some ensemble members bring the low \njust far enough north to push the warm sector into at least southern \nareas. This would cause some severe potential, so the threat will be \nwatched. GEFS/EPS/CMCE combined probabilities of receiving more than \nan inch of rain are under 10 percent, and probabilities of a half \ninch are from 10-30 percent. Overall, not much of a flood threat is \nexpected. Behind the system, temperatures should trend seasonably \ncool with a couple additional chances of showers.",
